<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Swift ${pc.foreign.state}</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-swift</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over bin/build-client.sh, which builds this module IN A CONTAINER: Swift
        is one of the two languages mise cannot serve here, because Swift.org publishes no Debian 13
        toolchain (astubbs#242,
        docs/inflight/parked-containerised-toolchains-and-runtime.md). The commands below run ONLY
        under -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for why
        opt-in is load-bearing - so an ordinary scoped build of this module starts no container at
        all.

        It calls the script rather than `swift` directly for the obvious reason: there is no swift on
        the host, and there will not be. The script owns which route a language takes; this pom only
        names the language. The test-phase invocation runs the EXTRACTED artifact on the host, which
        is what asserts the static link - see the script's header. (Its flag cannot be written in an
        XML comment: two consecutive hyphens end one.)

        The path is relative to this module rather than ${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}: that
        property is set by the mvn launcher and is empty when the build is driven by an IDE or an
        embedder, which would silently invoke "/bin/build-client.sh".

        THIS MODULE CONFIGURES NO maven-clean-plugin FILESET, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN AN
        OMISSION. Every other foreign client declares one, because its native toolchain writes
        somewhere Maven has never heard of; the container route writes exactly ONE host-side path,
        the export directory bin/build-client.sh computes as <module>/target/container (grep
        `out_dir` in that script). That is inside ${project.build.directory}, so the default clean
        already deletes the two extracted binaries, the dynamic control, Package.resolved and
        link-report.txt - by the same mechanism that keeps the promise for a Java module, with
        nothing added. Measured on this module: build, `target/container/pc-swift-selftest`
        present, `mvn clean`, gone, rebuild green.

        THERE IS NO `.build` DIRECTORY HERE TO DELETE, and the reason is worth knowing before
        someone adds a fileset for one. SwiftPM's three scratch paths are BuildKit CACHE MOUNTS
        inside the image (grep `pc-swift-scratch-static` in the Dockerfile), one per build
        configuration because two configurations sharing a path invalidate each other's whole
        dependency graph - measured at 151s of pure re-compilation per edit. They are shared build
        cache, not module output: deleting them would not remove one file from this directory, and
        would cost the next build a full rebuild of SwiftNIO and grpc-swift. The Dockerfile keeps
        them outside the package directory precisely so a stray `.build` can never appear in the
        checkout.

        WHAT MUST NOT BE ADDED HERE. Everything else this build produces is SHARED: the swift:6.1
        base image (3.4 GB), the BuildKit layer cache including the two compiled protoc plugins, and
        the apt and SwiftPM cache mounts. Several agents and worktrees run against this machine at
        once, so deleting any of them would throw away output nobody asked this module to rebuild,
        and would turn a colleague's 2-second cached build into a many-minute one.
        bin/build-client.sh's `build_natively` records the same reasoning for the native route.
        They are this language's ~/.m2, and `mvn clean` does not empty ~/.m2.

        And nothing here may list `target/container` explicitly either: that would be a second copy
        of the script's `out_dir`, free to drift from it, deleting something already deleted.

        Skeleton until the Swift wave of the language-proxy plan starts; that wave owns everything in
        this directory except the build environment (Dockerfile, toolchain-smoke/), and may replace
        the smoke target once it has one of its own.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>${project.basedir}/../../bin/build-client.sh</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>swift</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>${project.basedir}/../../bin/build-client.sh</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>swift --test</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
